        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The primary objective of this investigation was to study the effect of 0-G on lymphocyte proliferation in order to establish possible alterations in natural defense mechanisms against infective diseases during prolonged space missions. 
<P>
Lymphocytes from cosmonauts of spaceflights Soyuz 6, 7 and 8 and from astronauts of the Apollo-Soyuz mission showed a depression of their reactivity toward mitogens after flight. On the other hand, no effect was observed on lymphocytes from crewmembers on other Apollo missions. It is not clear whether the observed effect is due to the stress of spaceflight on the organism with consequent changes in the concentration of certain components of body fluids (e.g., calcium), or to an effect of weightlessness per se. Consequently, only the exposure of lymphocytes to mitogens in vitro during a spaceflight would give a clear answer to the question. 
<P>
The investigator hypothesized that the absence of gravity affects lymphocyte proliferation. This was tested using three major lines of investigation: <br>
<LI> the effect of stress and the effect of weightlessness per se on lymphocyte activation.
<LI> the kinetics of lymphocyte proliferation in space.
<LI> the effects of a gravitational environment between 0 and 2G. 
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
Culture preparation:  The activation by concanavalin A (ConA) of human peripheral blood lymphocytes in the presence of monocytes (as accessory cells) was investigated in cultures exposed to microgravity conditions in Spacelab.  Lymphocytes were drawn from the peripheral blood of one healthy male donor 24 hours before launch; they were purified by gradient centrifugation on Ficoll-Hypaque.  Differential counting showed that the preparation contained 84% small lymphocytes, with the remainder of the cells being 13% macrophages and polymorphonuclear cells.  Cell viability was 90%.  The cells were resuspended in RPMI 1640 medium supplemented with 40 mmol/L HEPES, 5 mmol/L sodium carbonate, 50 microg/L gentamycin, 20% heat activated fetal calf serum, and 4 mmol/L L-glutamine.  A 10 ml sample was sealed in each of 8 flasks; eight other flasks were preincubated for 4 hours at 37 degrees Celsius with MC beads, Cytodex 1 at a ratio of 100 cells/bead.  Therefore, 4 sets of 4 cultures were prepared 15 hours before launch and stored at room temperature in the Shuttle.  Two cultures in each set had MC beads and two did not.  Two sets were used as ground controls, one set was put in the flight incubator, and another set was put into the Low-G Centrifuge to serve as a flight control.
<P>
Flight operations:  Six hours after launch, the two flight sets of cultures were transferred to the incubator and centrifuge and were exposed to ConA.  One of the two ground sets was not treated with ConA.  Two cultures from each of the 4 sets (one with and one without MC beads) were treated with a 2-h pulse of 74 kBq/ml of tritiated thymidine 46 and 65 hours after injection of ConA.  Dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) at a final concentration of 10% was injected into the cultures before storage in the freezer.
<P>
Biochemical analyses:  Cell proliferation was determined by measuring:<br>
<LI> the rate of tritiated thymidine incorporated into DNA.  Triplicates (1 ml each) of each culture were used.
<LI> secretion of interleukin-2 (IL-2) and gamma interferon
<LI> expression of IL-2 receptors
<P>
Electron microscopy:  Postflight electron microscopy was performed on cells fixed inflight with DMSO.  After careful thawing postflight, the samples were fixed with glutaraldehyde and osmium tetroxide
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
Activation of resuspended T cells is strongly inhibited in space, a result also seen in earlier investigations.  However, activation of cells attached to microcarrier beads is more than doubled in microgravity. The results suggest that the depression of the activation in resuspended cells may be attributed to a malfunction of monocytes acting as accessory cells. In fact, although the ultrastructure of resuspended monocytes is not altered in microgravity, the secretion of IL-1 is strongly inhibited. The data suggest:<br>
<LI> IL-2 is produced independently of IL-1
<LI> IL-1 production is triggered only when monocytes adhere to microcarriers
<LI> the expression of IL-2 receptors depends on IL-1
<LI> provided sufficient IL-1 is available, activation is enhanced in microgravity.
<P>
Finally, cultures of resuspended peripheral blood lymphocytes and monocytes in microgravity constitute a complete and natural system in which monocytes are not operational. This may be useful for studies of the role of accessory cells and cell-cell interactions in T lymphocyte activation. <END>
